# The goldens are byte-exact fixtures: their bytes ARE the assertion.
# They are read with read_text, whose universal-newline translation
# hides a line-ending rewrite from the comparison, so a CRLF checkout
# passes for a reason unrelated to the emitter being right. Pinning
# them to LF makes the file on disk the same on every machine, which
# is what "byte-exact" has to mean before the phrase is worth writing.
# The whitespace pre-commit hooks are excluded from this directory for
# the same reason (.pre-commit-config.yaml), after one of them rewrote
# a golden's last byte on 2026-08-07.
tests/goldens/** text eol=lf

# The fixtures are the same argument as the goldens above, and they cost
# two tier-1 failures on 2026-08-19 before anyone made it. A fixture read
# as BYTES carries its line endings into the assertion, so an unpinned one
# is CRLF on Windows and LF on Linux and the case measures the checkout.
# Two cases did: one asserted the fixture was CRLF and fired its own
# non-vacuity guard on every Linux runner, the other split on a CRLF that
# was not there.
#
# THE FIRST VERSION OF THIS RULE WAS `-text`, ON A PREMISE THAT MEASURED
# FALSE, and the correction is kept here because the premise is the
# tempting one. It said several fixtures are captured solver output whose
# CRLF is what the solver wrote, so normalizing them would make the
# committed bytes disagree with the run they record. The measurement that
# settles it is `git ls-files --eol tests/fixtures`: every fixture is
# `i/lf`. The index has held LF all along, this machine's
# `core.autocrlf=true` is what puts a CR in the working tree, and the
# solver's CRLF, if it ever existed, was normalized away at the original
# `git add` years before any of this. Nothing was being preserved.
#
# `eol=lf` rather than `-text` for the reason that survives the
# correction: both write LF on checkout, and only this one keeps a CRLF
# OUT OF THE INDEX. Under `-text` a later `git add` of a working copy
# that had drifted to CRLF would commit the CR verbatim, changing eleven
# evidence files in a commit about something else. The guard beside it
# (tests/test_matrix.py) asserts the pin, not the absence of a CR.
tests/fixtures/** text eol=lf
